
  - [1mTime passing[0m — a task comes due, goes overdue, or a reminder you set arrives. This is the big one, and realistically
    most of what "notification" means here.
  - [1mRecurrence[0m — a repeating task reappears on its own, if we build recurrence.
  - [1mAnother tab[0m — you edited the same list in a second window. Real, but a sync detail rather than a notification.

  So there's a fork in what you're actually asking for, and it's worth being explicit because they're different features
  with different amounts of work. One is a scheduler; one is a history view; they share almost no code.

  - [1mReminders/due alerts[0m — a scheduler plus the browser Notification API. Genuinely tells you something you didn't know.
    Costs: permission prompts, and it only fires while a tab is open unless we add a service worker.
  - [1mActivity log[0m — a running "what changed when" list you can scroll. Cheap to build, useful for "wait, when did I move
    that?", but it never interrupts you, so it isn't really notification.
  - [1mBoth[0m — they're independent; either can come second without rework.

  [1mMy recommendation: reminders and due alerts.[0m It's the only one that tells you something you don't already know, and
  it's the feature that makes a task app actually useful rather than a list you forget to open.
